<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sijilmasa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sijilmasa was a medieval trading city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tafilalt">Tafilalt</a> oasis of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> and a northern terminus of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-trade">trans-Saharan gold</a> trade. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> captured it in the early 1050s, gaining control over the gold trade. Its first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a>, was proclaimed after being retrieved from Sijilmasa in 909.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sijilmasa.jpg" alt="Sijilmasa" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Loonybad · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1886608-the_ruins_of_Sijilmassa-Rissani.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sijilmasa was a medieval Moroccan city and trade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/entrepot">entrepôt</a> at the northern edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>. The ruins extend for five miles along the River Ziz in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tafilalt">Tafilalt</a> oasis near the town of Rissani. Much of its economic success has been attributed to the oasis's large canal. Until the 14th century, as the northern terminus of the western trans-Sahara trade route, it was one of the most important trade centres of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to al-Bakri, Sufrite Kharijites first settled the town after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berber-revolt">Berber revolts</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a>. When they numbered about four thousand they elected a leader, 'Isa bin Mazid al-Aswad, who after 14 years was accused of corruption and executed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-qasim-samgu">Abu al-Qasim Samgu</a> bin Wasul al-Miknasi then led the town, and he and his descendants are known as the Midrar dynasty. Its wealth let the city assert its independence from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> as early as 771. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-hawqal">Ibn Hawqal</a>, who visited between 947 and 951, reports a bill of 42,000 dinars issued to a trader at Awdaghust by a merchant of Sijilmasa, a sum he had never heard of elsewhere.</p><p>In 905 'Abd <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a> al-Mahdi Billah, later regarded as the founder of the Fatimid dynasty, arrived in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> with his son al-Qa'im, fleeing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a>, and hid in Sijilmasa for four years under the Midrar ruler Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yasa">Yasa</a>'. Yasa' imprisoned them. An Isma'ili army under Abu 'Abdallah reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tafilalt">Tafilalt</a> in the latter half of 909 and besieged the city. Yasa' was killed that year or the next, and the Midrar dynasty fragmented until the Maghrawa Berbers took the city. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdallah-ibn-yasin">Abdallah ibn Yasin</a>, made it their first conquest, and it was the site of their first mint and their only mint from 1058 to 1100. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a> took it in 1146.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> stayed there in 1352-1353 and praised its dates. By the early 16th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-africanus">Leo Africanus</a> found it destroyed, though its walls still stood. He reports that the last prince was assassinated by its citizens, whereas <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a> attributes its fall to a lack of resources. Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moulay-ismail">Moulay Ismail</a> had it rebuilt in the 18th century, and in 1818 the nomadic Ait Atta tribes conquered and destroyed it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The ruins lie one kilometre north of Rissani and are recognised by the World Monuments Fund as an endangered site. The Moroccan-American Project of Sijilmassa, launched in 1988, led to six excavations. The Moroccan Ministry of Culture preserves the ruins.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sijilmasa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>That year his forces brought Abdallah al-Mahdi from <b>Sijilmasa</b> to Ifriqiya and founded the Fatimid Caliphate.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Almoravids captured <b>Sijilmasa</b>, the northern terminus of the trans-Saharan gold trade, in 1054–1055, and soon afterwards Awdaghust in the south.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 909 his Kutama army overthrew the Aghlabid emirate, and the imam was retrieved from <b>Sijilmasa</b> and proclaimed caliph at Raqqada in January 910 as Abdallah al-Mahdi Billah, establishing a rival caliphate to Baghdad.</blockquote><a href="/empire/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The land was held by local Berber tribes and principalities such as the Barghwata Confederacy on the Atlantic coast and the Midrarid Emirate in <b>Sijilmasa</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/idrisid-dynasty">Idrisid dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 987 their chief Ziri ibn Atiyya took control of Fez as Córdoba's governor, while another branch of the tribe had seized <b>Sijilmasa</b> from the Midrarid emirate in 976.</blockquote><a href="/state/maghrawa-emirate-of-fez">Maghrawa emirate of Fez</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Sijilmasa</b> was founded in the Tafilalt oasis of south-eastern Morocco in 757 by Sufri Kharijite Berbers of the Miknasa tribe.</blockquote><a href="/state/midrarid-emirate">Midrarid emirate</a></li></ul></section>
